Assign every event above exactly one disposition, using these tools:

- `handle_one_event` — the event needs work and stands on its own. A capable
  subagent will be given the event and your instructions.
- `handle_event_sequence` — handling one of these events changes how another
  should be handled, so one subagent takes them in the order you list. The
  clearest case crosses streams: a job whose result answers a question someone
  asked somewhere else.
- `defer_event` — the event does not need attention now. Say why; it goes to the
  deferred backlog, which is swept separately.

Guidance:

- The priority says how the event arrived, not how much it matters. An
  `active` event is someone in a live exchange, and a `nudge` is something
  that was not to wait for your next heartbeat: handle both. An `async` event
  belongs to its stream's conversation and is for that conversation's agent to
  take up at its own pace, so hand it there. A `background` event is usually
  deferrable unless it has become stale enough to matter, or it unblocks
  something that is not.
- Group only what genuinely belongs together. Two unrelated messages from the
  same person are still two events.
- A `stream` is where an event happened — a room, a correspondent, a recurring
  job — not what it is about. Two events sharing a stream are a reason to look
  for a connection, not evidence of one; two consecutive messages from the same
  person are usually one piece of work, two unrelated pings in a busy room are
  not.
- Deferring is not dismissal and not a verdict on whether the event matters at
  all — that call belongs to the sweep, which sees the full history. Defer when
  the event does not need attention in this pass.

